Background
Arsenic exists naturally in the environment in various forms; some are toxic, some benign. The toxic forms
include inorganic species, and their partially detoxified
metabolites called monomethylarsonic acid (MMA) and
dimethylarsinic acid (DMA)1). The inorganic forms are dangerous to humans, and unfortunately symptoms of their toxicity are generally insidious and nonspecific in both severity
and time course2). Exposure to higher-than-average levels of
arsenic occurs mainly in workplaces, near or in hazardous
waste sites, and areas with high levels naturally occurring
in soil, rocks, and water. Globally, it is estimated that over
226 million people in 56 countries are exposed to unsafe
concentrations of arsenic found in their drinking water and
food3). The highest levels are found in the groundwater of

