of organs and have been linked to an increased
risk of cancer, infertility, brain damage, hearing
loss, and blindness, as well as a host of other major
health conditions, all of which might finally result
in the death of the person who is afflicted [3, 4].
Even at extremely low amounts, the majority
of the toxic metals dumped into the wastewater
have been determined to be poisonous, carcinogenic, and cause substantial health issues for human beings [5]. Cadmium, copper, and lead are
all elements that may often be found in aquatic
